{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 asserts resilience comes from messiness and inconsistent patching but never explains how independent failures are actually distinguished from merely uncorrelated ones. The problem context explicitly requires identifying evidence that distinguishes failure modes that are truly independent from failures that merely appear uncorrelated until a shared cause is found. SRC_001's 'Standardization Trap' mechanism describes why failures might be prevented but provides no causal account for how one would verify independence post-hoc versus discover a hidden shared cause. Its own counterconditions show this gap: a shared RFC standard creates a shared cause that is indistinguishable from 'true independence' under SRC_001's logic, because the diversity is only apparent. The reasoning envelope fails the problem's core ask because 'lack of optimization' cannot produce the evidence required to rule out latent correlation.","counterexample":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","gap":"SRC_001 explains why unoptimized systems might be diverse but cannot specify the evidence that would distinguish a genuinely independent failure from one caused by a common dependency (e.g., a shared RFC or library) — the exact evidence the problem asks it to identify."}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 claims central control provides superior resilience via active isolation and global load-shedding, but fails the problem's required task of identifying what evidence distinguishes truly independent failures from merely uncorrelated ones. SRC_002's causal mechanism (kill switch, traffic rerouting, coordinated retries) presupposes the ability to detect a correlation pattern — yet it never explains how to distinguish whether that pattern is a true correlated cascade (shared cause) or simply multiple independent failures occurring simultaneously that merely appear correlated. The 'poison pill' countercondition demonstrates the exact vulnerability: if every redundant node has identical software logic, the failure is identical in every node, meaning the apparent redundancy is illusory and the 'independence' was never there to begin with. SRC_002 cannot produce the evidence the problem demands because it treats any observed correlation as evidence for orchestration, without a mechanism to separate true independence from hidden shared causes.","counterexample":[{"target_alias":"SRC_002","gap":"SRC_002's kill-switch logic cannot, by itself, prove that the cascade it detects is caused by a shared root cause rather than a coincidence of independent failures; it therefore cannot supply the evidence that the problem asks it to identify."}]}]}